            <ArticleTitle>Screening outcomes of household contacts of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis patients in Peshawar, Pakistan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>909-912</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1995-7645(16)30154-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.apjtm.2016.07.017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the profile of TB/multidrug-resistant TB (MDR-TB) among household contacts of MDR-TB patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Close contacts of MDR-TB patients were traced in the cross-sectional study. Different clinical, radiological and bacteriological were performed to rule out the evidence of TB/MDR-TB.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Between January 2012 and December 2012, a total of 200 index MDR-TB patients were initiated on MDR-TB treatment, out of which home visit and contacts screening were conducted for 154 index cases. Of 610 contacts who could be studied, 41 (17.4%) were diagnosed with MDR-TB and 10 (4.2%) had TB. The most common symptoms observed were cough, chest pain and fever.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The high incidence of MDR-TB among close contacts emphasize the need for effective contact screening programme of index MDR-TB cases in order to cut the chain of transmission of this disease.</AbstractText>
